  "content": {
    "id": "p4-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "The points $A(-2,1)$, $B(1,3)$ and $C(7,-6)$ are joined to form a triangle. Prove that the triangle is right-angled.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$AB^2+BC^2=13+117=130=AC^2$, so the triangle is right-angled at $B$.\n\nUse the converse of Pythagoras’ theorem.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}AB^2&=(1-(-2))^2+(3-1)^2\\\\AB^2&=9+4=13\\\\BC^2&=(7-1)^2+(-6-3)^2\\\\BC^2&=36+81=117\\\\AC^2&=(7-(-2))^2+(-6-1)^2\\\\AC^2&=81+49=130\\\\AB^2+BC^2&=13+117=130\\\\AB^2+BC^2&=AC^2\\end{align*}$$\nBy the converse of Pythagoras’ theorem, triangle $ABC$ is right-angled at $B$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 56
  },
